    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>There is no best time: no published trial has compared taking collagen in the morning with the evening, or on an empty stomach with food.</strong> What the successful trials had in common was a full dose taken every day for 8 to 24 weeks, so the best time is the time you will not forget.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Empty stomach:</strong> the claim that fasting improves absorption has no trial behind it. Collagen peptides are absorbed in the small intestine whenever you take them, appearing in blood within one to two hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Before exercise:</strong> the one timing signal: in 8 men, 15g of vitamin C-enriched gelatin an hour before skipping roughly doubled a collagen-synthesis marker. A small biomarker study, not proof of fewer injuries.</li>
        <li><strong>Before bed:</strong> 3g of glycine before bed improved sleep quality in small Japanese trials. Collagen is rich in glycine, but no trial has tested collagen itself for sleep.</li>
        <li><strong>What matters more:</strong> the dose, taking it daily, and enough vitamin C in your diet.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

