      <h2 id="empty">Should you take collagen on an empty stomach?</h2>

      <p>
        It does not appear to matter. The empty-stomach advice usually rests on the idea that collagen
        will compete with other protein for absorption, but no trial has shown that this changes any
        outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen peptides are absorbed in the small intestine whatever the time of day. Absorption studies,
        including a 2024 crossover study in healthy adults published in <em>Frontiers in Nutrition</em>,
        show collagen-derived amino acids and peptides such as hydroxyproline rising in the blood quickly
        after a single dose, with some peptide forms still measurable hours later. If collagen on an empty
        stomach upsets your stomach or gives you reflux, take it with food. Comfort will keep you taking
        it, and that matters more than any theoretical absorption edge.
      </p>

      <h2 id="exercise">Should you take collagen before a workout?</h2>

      <p>
        This is the one place timing has some evidence, and it is small. If tendons or ligaments are your
        concern, taking collagen about an hour before training is a reasonable choice.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2017 study in the <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> gave 8 healthy men 5g or 15g of
        gelatin enriched with 48mg of vitamin C, or placebo, one hour before six minutes of rope-skipping,
        three times a day for three days. On 15g, a blood marker of collagen synthesis called PINP roughly
        doubled. The amino acids from the gelatin peaked in the blood an hour after dosing, which is why
        the timing was chosen: to have the building blocks circulating when exercise loads the tissue.
      </p>

      <p>
        The limits are real. It was 8 men, it measured a blood marker rather than injury rates or tendon
        strength, and it used gelatin, a less broken-down form than the hydrolysed peptides in most
        supplements. An accompanying editorial welcomed the idea but cautioned against overreading it. It
        is a plausible reason to take your dose before training, not proof that doing so prevents injury.
        Skelecoll provides 10,000mg of hydrolysed collagen with 40mg of vitamin C, in the same ballpark as
        the vitamin C in that study.
      </p>

