      <h2 id="night">Does collagen at night help sleep?</h2>

      <p>
        Not proven. The claim borrows from glycine research. Collagen is unusually rich in glycine, and
        small trials, mostly from one research group in Japan, found that 3g of glycine taken before bed
        improved subjective sleep quality and reduced next-day fatigue in poor sleepers.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2023 review in <em>GeroScience</em> summarised those studies as small and short. More
        importantly, they tested glycine on its own, not collagen, and no trial has tested collagen for
        sleep. Taking collagen at night is perfectly fine if that is when you remember it. Expecting it to
        work as a sleep aid goes beyond the evidence.
      </p>

      <h2 id="routine">How to build a routine that works</h2>


      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">If your goal is</th><th scope="col">A sensible time</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Skin, hair or nails</th><td>Whenever you will not forget</td><td>No timing evidence; daily consistency is what the trials controlled</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Tendons, ligaments, training</th><td>About an hour before training</td><td>The one small timing study used this window</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Joint comfort</th><td>Same time every day, with or without food</td><td>No timing evidence; aim for 12 weeks without gaps</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">A sensitive stomach</th><td>With a meal</td><td>Comfort keeps you taking it</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Attach it to a fixed habit,</strong> such as morning tea or brushing your teeth. Habit beats the clock.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep vitamin C in your diet.</strong> It is required for the body to make collagen. Amla, guava and citrus are easy sources.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not double up after a missed day.</strong> Just continue the next day.</li>
        <li><strong>Judge it after 12 weeks,</strong> not after the first fortnight.</li>
      </ol>

