      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best time to take collagen?</summary>
          <p>There is no proven best time. No trial has compared morning with evening, or empty stomach with food. The positive trials had people take a full dose every day for 8 to 24 weeks, so choose the time you will remember.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take collagen in the morning or at night?</summary>
          <p>Either. Collagen peptides are absorbed whenever you take them. Taking it at the same time every day matters more than which time you choose.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it better to take collagen on an empty stomach?</summary>
          <p>There is no evidence that it is. If collagen on an empty stomach causes discomfort or reflux, take it with food. Staying consistent matters more than any theoretical absorption advantage.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take collagen before or after a workout?</summary>
          <p>If tendons or ligaments are your concern, about an hour before. In a small study of 8 men, 15g of vitamin C-enriched gelatin an hour before exercise roughly doubled a blood marker of collagen synthesis. It did not measure injuries.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does taking collagen before bed help you sleep?</summary>
          <p>It has not been tested. Small trials found 3g of glycine before bed improved sleep quality, and collagen is rich in glycine, but no trial has tested collagen itself for sleep.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen with coffee or tea?</summary>
          <p>Yes. No study shows that coffee or tea stops collagen peptides being absorbed, and pairing collagen with a morning drink is an easy way to make it a daily habit.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take collagen with vitamin C?</summary>
          <p>Your body needs vitamin C to make collagen, so make sure your diet includes it. Skelecoll contains 40mg and Collabloom 250mg of vitamin C from amla, and fruit such as amla, guava and oranges easily covers the rest.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

